{"id":"7a868e38343f1a03ab49d830359e80b0ff147ca2","axis":"silent","repo":"apache/druid","sha":"7a868e38343f1a03ab49d830359e80b0ff147ca2","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"docs/configuration/index.md","lines":["@@ -235,6 +235,7 @@ values for the above mentioned configs among others provided by Java implementat"," |`druid.auth.unsecuredPaths`| List of Strings|List of paths for which security checks will not be performed. All requests to these paths will be allowed.|[]|no|"," |`druid.auth.allowUnauthenticatedHttpOptions`|Boolean|If true, skip authentication checks for HTTP OPTIONS requests. This is needed for certain use cases, such as supporting CORS pre-flight requests. Note that disabling authentication checks for OPTIONS requests will allow unauthenticated users to determine what Druid endpoints are valid (by checking if the OPTIONS request returns a 200 instead of 404), so enabling this option may reveal information about server configuration, including information about what extensions are loaded (if those extensions add extensions).|false|no|"," |`druid.auth.emitAuthMetrics`|Boolean|If true, emit service metrics for authorization events. When enabled, Druid emits `auth/forbidden` when access is denied and `auth/exception` when an internal authorization error occurs. See [Security metrics](#security) for details.|false|no|","+|`druid.auth.enableLookupAuthorization`|Boolean|If true, authorize lookup endpoints with `LOOKUP` resources. Requests where neither a tier nor a lookup can be resolved require `LOOKUP:LOOKUP`. See [LOOKUP resource permissions](../operations/security-user-auth.md#lookup).|false|no|"," "," For more information, please see [Authentication and Authorization](../operations/auth.md)."," "],"additions":1,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"docs/operations/security-user-auth.md","lines":["@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@ Druid uses the following resource types:"," * DATASOURCE &ndash; Each Druid table (i.e., `tables` in the `druid` schema in SQL) is a resource."," * CONFIG &ndash; Configuration resources exposed by the cluster components. "," * EXTERNAL &ndash; External data read through the [EXTERN function](../multi-stage-query/concepts.md#read-external-data-with-extern) in SQL.","+* LOOKUP &ndash; Lookup configuration, status, and introspection endpoints when `druid.auth.enableLookupAuthorization` is true."," * STATE &ndash; Cluster-wide state resources."," * SYSTEM_TABLE &ndash; when the Broker property `druid.sql.planner.authorizeSystemTablesDirectly` is true, then Druid uses this resource type to authorize the system tables in the `sys` schema in SQL."," ","@@ -85,6 +86,20 @@ Resource names for this type are datasource names. Specifying a datasource permi"," Any authenticated user can read the contents of any [lookup](../querying/lookups.md). Avoid storing sensitive data in lookups."," :::"," ","+### `LOOKUP`","+","+When `druid.auth.enableLookupAuthorization` is true, lookup endpoints authorize against `LOOKUP` resources named `tier/lookup`. The tier comes from the URL, bulk request body, coordinator lookup state or the serving node. `READ` covers GET requests, including lookup configuration, status, node status and introspection. `WRITE` covers POST, PUT and DELETE requests.","+","+In resource names, `%` and `/` in each tier or lookup name are encoded as `%25` and `%2F` before the parts are joined. For example, lookup `x/y` in tier `a` uses `LOOKUP:a/x%2Fy`, while lookup `y` in tier `a/x` uses `LOOKUP:a%2Fx/y`.","+","+The setting is false by default, so existing lookup endpoints continue to use `CONFIG:CONFIG` until it is enabled. Before enabling it with Druid basic security, add `LOOKUP` `READ` and `WRITE` on `.*` to the existing permissions for the `admin` and `druid_system` roles. Existing roles are not updated automatically when the resource type is added.","+","+This setting applies to lookup HTTP endpoints. It does not currently change lookup use in SQL or native queries, i.e., query execution does not check `LOOKUP` permissions.","+","+Bulk GET responses omit lookups for which the caller lacks `READ` authorization. To list an empty tier, grant `READ` on `LOOKUP:<tier>`.","+","+Bulk writes check `WRITE` on every affected lookup before making changes. Deleting an empty tier checks `WRITE` on `LOOKUP:<tier>`. An empty coordinator-wide write, such as `POST /druid/coordinator/v1/lookups/config` with `{}`, checks `WRITE` on `LOOKUP:LOOKUP`.","+"," ### `CONFIG`"," There are two possible resource names for the \"CONFIG\" resource type, \"CONFIG\" and \"security\". Granting a user access to CONFIG resources allows them to access the following endpoints."," "],"additions":15,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"server/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/query/lookup/LookupIntrospectionResource.java","lines":["@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@"," import com.google.inject.Inject;"," import com.sun.jersey.spi.container.ResourceFilters;"," import org.apache.druid.java.util.common.logger.Logger;","-import org.apache.druid.server.http.security.ConfigResourceFilter;","+import org.apache.druid.server.http.security.LookupResourceFilter;"," "," import javax.ws.rs.Path;"," import javax.ws.rs.PathParam;","@@ -31,7 +31,7 @@"," import java.util.Optional;"," "," @Path(\"/druid/v1/lookups/introspect\")","-@ResourceFilters(ConfigResourceFilter.class)","+@ResourceFilters(LookupResourceFilter.class)"," public class LookupIntrospectionResource"," {","   private static final Logger LOGGER = new Logger(LookupIntrospectionResource.class);"],"additions":2,"deletions":2,"truncated":false},{"path":"server/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/query/lookup/LookupListeningResource.java","lines":["@@ -23,25 +23,40 @@"," import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;"," import com.google.common.collect.ImmutableMap;"," import com.google.inject.Inject;","+import com.google.inject.Provider;"," import com.sun.jersey.spi.container.ResourceFilters;","+import org.apache.druid.discovery.LookupNodeService;"," import org.apache.druid.guic